<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  DEBUG ONLY (P2-21).

  Android has blocked cleartext HTTP by default since API 28, and the Local
  Emulator Suite speaks plain HTTP. Without this the platform branch is correct
  — the app resolves the right host — and the connection is refused anyway,
  with an error naming cleartext rather than the host, which is a confusing
  thing to reach from a failed sign-in.

  This file lives under src/debug/, so it is merged into debug builds ONLY.
  Release builds inherit Android's default and cannot talk cleartext to
  anything. `android_network_config_test.dart` fails if that stops being true.

  ===========================================================================
  WHY base-config RATHER THAN A LIST OF HOSTS

  This started as three <domain> entries — 10.0.2.2, 127.0.0.1, localhost —
  which covers the Android emulator and loopback but NOT a real device, which
  reaches the host on a LAN address like 172.20.10.3. `EMULATOR_HOST` exists
  precisely to name such an address, so the narrow config contradicted the very
  flag it was meant to support. Found on a physical SM-A325F:

      [emulator] host=172.20.10.3 explicit=true
      E/RecaptchaCallWrapper: Cleartext HTTP traffic to 172.20.10.3 not permitted

  Listing the private ranges instead is NOT POSSIBLE. <domain> matches
  HOSTNAMES — exactly, or with subdomains. There is no CIDR or wildcard form,
  so "192.168.0.0/16" would be parsed as a literal hostname and match nothing.
  It would look like a fix and silently keep failing, which is worse than the
  bug.

  Generating the entry at build time from the dart-define was the other
  candidate. Gradle can read `-Pdart-defines` (base64 CSV), so it is genuinely
  possible — but it needs a task ordered against mergeDebugResources, and it
  yields an empty or invalid config on every run that omits the define, which
  is every simulator and emulator run. That is a lot of fragile machinery
  guarding a debug-only affordance.

  So: permit cleartext generally and let src/debug/ be the boundary. The scope
  that matters here is the BUILD TYPE, not the host list.

  ===========================================================================
  PREFER `adb reverse` TO RELYING ON THIS

  For a USB-connected device — which you need anyway to install a debug build:

      adb -s <id> reverse tcp:8080 tcp:8080
      adb -s <id> reverse tcp:9099 tcp:9099
      adb -s <id> reverse tcp:5001 tcp:5001
      flutter run -d <id> --dart-define=EMULATOR_HOST=localhost

  The device reaches the suite on its own loopback: no LAN, no 0.0.0.0 binding
  in firebase.json (D-25), nothing that breaks when the network moves. This
  file is the fallback for when tunnelling is not available.

  One trap, because it briefly convinced me adb reverse was impossible: the
  Firebase plugins default automaticHostMapping TRUE, which on Android rewrites
  localhost and 127.0.0.1 to 10.0.2.2 before connecting. main.dart passes false
  on all three. If loopback ever stops working from a handset, look for
  `Mapping Firestore Emulator host "localhost" to "10.0.2.2"` in the device log
  before blaming the tunnel. See docs/local-run.md.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
